{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "R4ALL_SOP_FairDataAssessment.pdf", "element_type": "CompositeElement", "page": 3, "doc_id": "d7633643-0dc9-4a47-9179-87e6d22b5514"}, "page_content": "[Source: R4ALL_SOP_FairDataAssessment.pdf]\n\nNomination of a dataset\n\nThe first step in evaluating a dataset's FAIRness level is to identify and nominate a dataset. In this context, a dataset is a collection of files that represent information in a predefined format and capture data within a similar context. This selected dataset then becomes the basis for the FAIR assessment and subsequent improvement steps. Examples of datasets include an Excel sheet containing experimental endpoint results, such as IC50 values for a compound library, or an analysis report in PDF or Word format from an instrument-based analysis.\n\nIdentification of stakeholders\n\nThe FAIR-DSM is a questionnaire-based system that enables data owners and analysts to quickly assess the FAIR maturity level of their dataset. The questions cover various aspects of data management, data generation, and legal requirements. Therefore, it is crucial to involve all relevant stakeholders in the evaluation process.\n\nTypically, the stakeholder group includes:\n\n\u2022 Data Managers: Experts in data handling and knowledgeable about the legal bindings related to the data.\n\n\u2022 Data Owners: Individuals responsible for generating the data.\n\n\u2022 Data Scientists: Professionals responsible for downstream analysis of the data.\n\n\u2022 Project Managers or Decision-Makers: Individuals who can provide the long-term vision for the dataset under evaluation.\n\nBy bringing together these diverse stakeholders, the FAIR-DSM ensures a comprehensive evaluation, addressing all facets necessary for improving the dataset's FAIRness.\n\nPre-FAIRification assessment\n\nOnce the required stakeholders have gathered, the next step is to go through the FAIR-DSM questionnaire (https://fairdsm.biospeak.solutions/) and provide answers to the best of their knowledge, keeping the selected dataset in context. The questions are in a multiple-choice format, and selecting the most appropriate answers, or all that apply, is recommended.\n\nSince the questions may contain technical and FAIR-specific terms, helper text is provided for each option to aid in understanding (Figure 3). For users interested in deeper insights, an\n\nR4ALL - SOP \u2013 FAIR data assessment\n\nPage: 4/9\n\nexplanation of how each question relates to the FAIR-DSM model is also available. This comprehensive approach ensures that non-FAIR experts can also use the tool.\n\nHosting Environment Ca apabilities Focus: Dataset Representation > More Info [2 of 17] Which of the following statements best describe how the shareable Dataset(s) are modeled and represented? \u00a9 Dataset(s) are standardised to a locally defined Dataset Model suitable for data sharing and re-use\u201d) \u00a9 Dataset(s) are standardised to relevant Domain or Community Standard Dataset Model(s) suitable for data sharing and re-use (?) \u00a9 Dataset(s) are standardised to a defined Semantic Data Model and represented using Linked Data Represetations suitable for data sharing and re-use\u201d) O Dataset(s) intended for sharing and re-use are granularly standardised and managed at the Data Element Level (e.g. ISO 11179 MDR standard)\\\u201d) C)None of the above < Previous Next > Submit\n\nFigure 3: An example question from the FAIR-DSM.\n\nThe FAIR-DSM comprises a total of 17 questions. Using smart approaches, only questions relevant to the answers provided will be asked, streamlining the process. Overall, this evaluation takes approximately 30-45 minutes. Upon completion of all relevant questions, the FAIR-DSM generates a report that demonstrates the metric for each FAIR Maturity Level. This metric helps researchers identify the current maturity level of their dataset. For instance, as shown in Figure 4, the current maturity level of the dataset might be Level 0. This report can also be downloaded for internal recording.\n\nLeve Level 1 Level 2 Level 3 Level 4 Level 5 Representation & Format 67% 38% 0% 0% 0% Content & Hosting Context Environment Capabilities 100% 100% 78% 75% 0% 60% 0% 67% 0% 0% Overall Level % Completion 86% 62% 15% 14% 0%\n\nFigure 4: An example of the final high-level report generated by the FAIR-DSM.\n\nR4ALL - SOP \u2013 FAIR data assessment\n\nPage: 5/9\n\nAlongside the high-level overview of the current dataset maturity level, the FAIR-DSM provides detailed insights into the criteria that either enable or hinder the dataset's progress towards higher Maturity Levels. For instance, in Figure 5, criteria are color-coded to indicate their status within the dataset: green signifies criteria that are currently satisfied, while red highlights those that are lacking. Achieving all criteria marked in red would elevate the dataset to Maturity Level 1. Furthermore, each criterion is categorized into three dimensions\u2014represented by blue sections\u2014 namely, R (representation), C (content), and H (hosting). These dimensions help researchers pinpoint specific areas within the dataset that require attention to enhance its FAIRness level.\n\nBased on this assessment, 2 indicators still need to be satisfied for your Datasets to reach Maturity Level 1 [DSM-1-R2]Structured and/or Unstructured Data are organised into Dataset(s) created for the purpose of FAIR sharing [DSM-1-RO]Dataset Metadata is formally represented in the form of an Identifiable Dataset Descriptor [DSM-1-R3]A representation of the Dataset Descriptor conforming to a relevant General Purpose Metadata Schema is available [DSM-1-R1]Contextual Metadata is reported at summary level and represented in the Dataset Descriptor [DSM-1-R4]Dataset Descriptor is available in Machine Readable Format [DSM-1-R5]Dataset(s) available in Machine Readable Format [DSM-1-CO]Each Dataset purposed for FAIR sharing is assigned a unique identifier [DSM-1-C1]Dataset Descriptor includes Descriptive Study/Project-Level summary information [DSM-1-C2]Dataset Descriptor includes Identifying & Descriptive Dataset-Level metadata [DSM-1-C3]Dataset Descriptor contains access information for the Dataset [DSM-1-H1]Metadata hosting environment stores and maintains an identifiable Dataset Descriptor for each identifiable Dataset [DSM-1-H2]The Dataset and its Descriptor are indexed and retrievable (in the same or separate hosting environments) via unique and persistent identifiers [DSM-1-H3]Retrieval of the Dataset and the Dataset Descriptor utilises a standardized communication protocol that is open, free and universally implementable [DSM-1-H4]Metadata hosting environment offers the capability to browse and search contents of the Dataset Descriptor\n\nFigure 5: An example of the maturity level detailed report generated by the FAIR-DSM.\n\nFinally, the pre-FAIR assessment of the dataset is conducted to determine its current maturity level and establish future goals for achieving higher levels of maturity. This assessment also aids in identifying potential technical and non-technical tasks necessary to reach these goals.\n[Figure] Screenshot of question 2/17 under \"Focus: Dataset Representation\" asking how shareable datasets are modeled and represented. It shows five checkbox options: standardised to a local dataset model; to a domain/community standard model; to a semantic data model using linked data; granular data-element level standardisation (e.g., ISO 11179); and \"None of the above.\" Navigation buttons \"Previous,\" \"Next,\" and \"Submit\" appear at the bottom.\n[Figure] Table of maturity levels showing percent completion (percent unit) across four categories: Representation & Format, Content & Context, Hosting Environment Capabilities, and Overall Level % Completion. Level 1 is most complete (67%, 100%, 100%, 86%); Level 2 is moderate (38%, 78%, 75%, 62%); Levels 3\u20135 show minimal progress (Level 3: 0%, 0%, 60%, 15%; Level 4: 0%, 0%, 67%, 14%; Level 5: 0% across all columns).\n[Figure] Assessment checklist for DSM\u20111 showing two unmet indicators (two red, unchecked lines about organising data into dataset(s) and formally representing dataset metadata) while the remaining DSM\u20111 criteria are green/checked (contextual and machine\u2011readable descriptors, identifiers, access and hosting requirements). Header states \"2 indicators still need to be satisfied for your Datasets to reach Maturity Level 1.\"", "type": "Document"}}